{
  "chapter": "security/prompt-injection",
  "tier": "drill",
  "notes": [
    "Taint is assigned by the fetcher, from where the bytes came from, never from anything",
    "about the bytes. There is no detection step, so there is nothing to craft around: a",
    "perfectly innocent ticket taints the run exactly as a hostile one does.",
    "Taint is monotonic. No later read clears it and no string the model produces reaches",
    "the flag.",
    "A tainted run may reach the low tool classes freely. Above the ceiling it needs a",
    "narrow, argument-constrained call: this ticket's own order, under the tier-0 cap.",
    "Blast radius lives in the arguments, and so does the injection's leverage.",
    "The recipient of an exfiltrating tool comes from the ticket record, not from the model,",
    "tainted or not. That is a control rather than a response to taint.",
    "The trifecta is reported and never enforced. It is a review checklist: a path can have",
    "all three and still be safe, because the vector was narrowed rather than removed."
  ],
  "config": { "maxClassWhenTainted": 2, "tier0CapCents": 100000 },
  "catalogue": {
    "get_order": { "class": 1, "exfiltrates": false },
    "search_policies": { "class": 1, "exfiltrates": false },
    "get_credit": { "class": 2, "exfiltrates": false },
    "issue_credit": { "class": 4, "exfiltrates": false },
    "send_reply": { "class": 5, "exfiltrates": true }
  },
  "cases": [
